INSERT INTO "messages" ("meta","seq","role","session_id","content","tool_calls","reasoning","inserted_at","id") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8,$9) [%{}, 3, :assistant, "a3b7f72c-b28f-4994-a8b9-ea0929558b91", "\n\nThe bug is clear: `String.to_integer/1` doesn't trim whitespace and raises on garbage instead of returning `:error`. I'll switch to trimming + `Integer.parse/1`, which returns `:error` on garbage and the remainder for trailing garbage.\n\n", [%{"arguments" => %{"new_string" => "  def parse(string) do\n    case string |> String.trim() |> Integer.parse() do\n      {int, \"\"} -> int\n      _ -> :error\n    end\n  end", "old_string" => "  def parse(string) do\n    String.to_integer(string)\n  end", "path" => "solution.ex"}, "id" => "chatcmpl-tool-94efcd7a47c677b8", "name" => "edit"}], "The module should:\n- Trim surrounding whitespace\n- Parse base-10 integer\n- Return :error when trimmed string is not exactly one integer (trailing garbage is error)\n- `String.to_integer/2` raises on garbage; `Integer.parse` is what we want.\n\nThe bug: it doesn't trim, and it raises instead of returning :error. Let me use `Integer.parse/1`.\n\n`Integer.parse(\"42\")` → `{42, \"\"}`. `Integer.parse(\"4a\")` → `{4, \"a\"}` — trailing garbage should be error. `Integer.parse(\"\")` → `:error`. `Integer.parse(\"-7\")` → `{-7, \"\"}`.\n\nBut note `Integer.parse` with whitespace inside... we trim first. After trim, \"  -7 \" → \"-7\" → `{-7, \"\"}`. Good.\n\nWhat about \"+5\"? Integer.parse(\"+5\") → `{5, \"\"}`. Spec doesn't mention, fine.\n\nWhat about \"\" trimmed → \"\" → Integer.parse(\"\") → :error. Good.\n\nLet me write the fix minimally.\n\n```elixir\ndef parse(string) do\n  case string |> String.trim() |> Integer.parse() do\n    {int, \"\"} -> int\n    _ -> :error\n  end\nend\n```\n\nThis keeps module name and public API (parse/1) intact. Minimal change. Let me apply and test.\n", ~U[2026-09-11 07:35:25.860150Z], "19c9d0bd-263d-4850-ab54-ea1f4f1b2478"]
