INSERT INTO "messages" ("meta","seq","role","session_id","tool_calls","reasoning","inserted_at","id") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8) [%{}, 7, :assistant, "f4ccd4bb-d017-48da-bdbb-1171cb21bc88", [%{"arguments" => %{"command" => "cd \"$(pwd)\" && cat > scratch/probe.exs <<'EOF'\nCode.require_file(\"solution.ex\")\ntry do Pricing.total(1, 10.5) catch e -> IO.inspect({:a, e}, label: \"float unit\") end\ntry do Pricing.total(1, 1000, 101) catch e -> IO.inspect({:b, e}, label: \"pct101\") end\ntry do Pricing.total(1, 1000, 10.5) catch e -> IO.inspect({:c, e}, label: \"floatpct\") end\nEOF\nelixir scratch/probe.exs"}, "id" => "chatcmpl-tool-91b31d563e7d4518", "name" => "bash"}], "Interesting. `Pricing.total(1, 1000, 101)` raised with ArithmeticError... no wait. The output: \"qty0 shipping waived: 0\" printed, then a crash for the next line. Actually the crash is the `try do Pricing.total(1, 1000, 101)` — wait no, the discount_pct=101 should fail the guard and raise FunctionClauseError, which would be caught by try. But the error shows ArithmeticError `div(0.0, 100)` at solution.ex:18.\n\nHmm, that's weird. Wait — the crash happened at test.exs:7 which is `try do Pricing.total(1, 1000, 101)`. But why ArithmeticError instead of FunctionClauseError? \n\nOh! The default argument. When I call `Pricing.total(1, 1000, 101)` directly with 3 args and 101 fails guard, it should raise FunctionClauseError caught by try. But it raised ArithmeticError with div(0.0,100)... \n\nWait actually let me reconsider. The guard order in Elixir: with multiple `when` clauses joined... all guards must pass for the function to match. 101 fails `discount_pct <= 100`, so no clause matches → FunctionClauseError. That should be caught.\n\nBut the error is ArithmeticError at line 18 (the div line) with 0.0. That means a call reached the body with discount that made... div(0.0, 100). Hmm, gross * discount_pct where gross is... 0.0? That means quantity or unit_cents was 0.0 (a float). \n\nWait, which call is on test.exs:7? Let me count lines. Line1 Code.require_file, line2 no discount, line3 10%, line4 qty0, line5 try 101, line6 try neg, line7 try float unit. So test.exs:7 is `Pricing.total(1, 10.5)`. The unit 10.5 is a float. Guard `is_integer(unit_cents) and unit_cents >= 0` — is_integer(10.5) is false → should fail guard → FunctionClauseError.\n\nBut it gave ArithmeticError div(0.0, 100)! That means the guard did NOT prevent matching?? \n\nHmm, wait. The issue: `div(gross * discount_pct, 100)` where gross = 1 * 10.5 = 10.5, discount_pct=0 → 10.5*0 = 0.0, div(0.0, 100) = ArithmeticError. So the body executed → the guard passed?! \n\nThat means `is_integer(10.5)` returned... no. Unless the multiple-`when` guard syntax isn't ANDing all. Let me reconsider: Actually in Elixir, when you write a head with multiple `when`, e.g. `def f(a,b) when g1 when g2`, hmm. The correct way to combine is `def f(a,b) when g1 and g2`. Multiple top-level `when`? Let me recall: `def foo(x) when is_integer(x) when x > 0` — I believe this is actually valid and treated as... Actually I'm not 100% sure. Historically `when` repeated is parsed; guards list is `when g1 when g2` which... Hmm.\n\nLet me recall: Elixir allows `when` only once at top level of a head, with `and`/`or` to combine. But there's also the rule that you can write `def foo(x) when is_atom(x) ... ` repeated `when`? Actually the parse of `def foo(x) when g1 when g2` → I think Elixir DOES treat multiple `when` as conjunction? Let me check. The error suggests the float guard didn't reject. Actually wait — maybe the body ran because the FIRST when `is_integer(quantity) and quantity >= 0` passed, and then the second/third weren't applied because of how default args + guard clauses interact? \n\nHmm, actually default arguments and guards: Elixir desugars `def total(a,b,c \\\\ 0) when ...` by creating a `total(a,b)` that calls `total(a,b,0)`, but the guard applies to the 3-arity. But there's a subtlety: when there are multiple clauses with defaults... only one clause here.\n\nLet me just test directly what happens.\n", ~U[2026-09-11 07:36:45.809957Z], "1cb7a8eb-c431-4fb4-a59a-6c64976e2706"]
