INSERT INTO "messages" ("meta","seq","role","session_id","tool_calls","reasoning","inserted_at","id") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8) [%{}, 15, :assistant, "f4ccd4bb-d017-48da-bdbb-1171cb21bc88", [%{"arguments" => %{"command" => "cd \"$(pwd)\" && cat > scratch/test2.exs <<'EOF'\nCode.require_file(\"solution.ex\")\nr = fn f -> try do {:ok, f.()} catch :error, e -> {:raised, elem(e,0)} end end\nIO.inspect(r.(fn -> Pricing.total(2,1000) end), label: \"no disc\")\nIO.inspect(r.(fn -> Pricing.total(2,1000,10) end), label: \"10%\")\nIO.inspect(r.(fn -> Pricing.total(0,1000,50) end), label: \"qty0\")\nIO.inspect(r.(fn -> Pricing.total(0,0,0) end), label: \"all0\")\nIO.inspect(r.(fn -> Pricing.total(1,1000,101) end), label: \"pct101\")\nIO.inspect(r.(fn -> Pricing.total(1,1000,-1) end), label: \"pctneg\")\nIO.inspect(r.(fn -> Pricing.total(-1,1000) end), label: \"negq\")\nIO.inspect(r.(fn -> Pricing.total(1,10.5) end), label: \"floatunit\")\nIO.inspect(r.(fn -> Pricing.total(1,1000,10.5) end), label: \"floatpct\")\nIO.inspect(r.(fn -> Pricing.total(\"a\",1000) end), label: \"strq\")\nEOF\nelixir scratch/test2.exs"}, "id" => "chatcmpl-tool-98a10721c97c29a7", "name" => "bash"}], "Now 101 raises FunctionClauseError, but my `try ... catch` doesn't catch it because `catch` only catches throws/exits/throws errors — actually `try/catch` catches `throw`, `exit`, and... to catch errors you need `rescue` or `catch :error, e`. In Elixir `try/catch` does not catch exceptions raised via `raise` (errors) unless you use `catch :error, e`. That's why my test crashes instead of catching. That's a test-harness issue, not a bug. The raise behavior is correct: invalid inputs raise FunctionClauseError. Good.\n\nLet me fix the test to confirm the remaining raise cases behave, using catch :error.\n", ~U[2026-09-11 07:37:15.789882Z], "785cc564-b332-4c9f-917a-df0fd50f6af0"]
