INSERT INTO "messages" ("meta","seq","role","session_id","tool_calls","reasoning","inserted_at","id") VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5,$6,$7,$8) [%{}, 17, :assistant, "1b57b9a9-1fe8-466c-8f06-1496a940271e", [%{"arguments" => %{"new_string" => "Code.require_file(\"solution.ex\")\n\ncases = [", "old_string" => "cases = [", "path" => "scratch/test.exs"}, "id" => "chatcmpl-tool-aa80d8050cd786ce", "name" => "edit"}], "Hmm, the file has 1 empty line. That's suspicious — it seems the harness injects that round marker into stdout of bash?? It seems my bash output is being replaced/appended with the round marker. And the elixir output isn't showing at all.\n\nMaybe elixir is buffering... no, exit=0 and file empty means the script didn't run. Perhaps `elixir solution.ex scratch/test.exs` loads solution.ex and treats `scratch/test.exs` as an *argument* to solution.ex rather than running it? Indeed, elixir's CLI: files given are compiled/run in order... Let me check: `elixir file.exs args` — the elixir binary's main function: it calls `elixir.start` which calls `elixir_distribution`... In `lib/elixir/lib/elixir.ex`, `start` handles argv: `parse_opts` — non-option args go to `state.argv`, and only the first non-option file is executed. Yes: \"The first argument is treated as a file to execute, rest are args.\" So solution.ex ran (defining module, no output) and test.exs was passed as :stdio args.\n\nUse `elixir -r solution.ex scratch/test.exs`? `-r` requires files. Let me do: `elixir -pa . -r solution.ex scratch/test.exs`. Actually `-r file` requires compiled .ex? `-r` loads files. Better: in the test.exs, use `Code.require_file(\"solution.ex\")` then run tests, and invoke `elixir scratch/test.exs`.\n", ~U[2026-09-11 07:47:06.947184Z], "d5174168-54af-447f-a4cf-e356b5a3023e"]
